              "image_path": "images/page_5_table_5.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td colspan=\"4\">Explanation with Algebra and Visualisation</td><td>Examples 4, 12, 16, 24,</td></tr><tr><td>Adding two (even) numbers that are multiples of 4 will always give a multiple of 4.</td><td>4p and 4q. <eq>4 p + 4 q</eq> <eq>= 4 \\: ( p + q ) .</eq></td><td><img src=\"images/page_5_table_image_5_1.jpg\"/> <img src=\"images/page_5_table_image_5_2.jpg\"/></td><td></td><td>36. <eq>\\begin{array}{l}12 + 16 \\\\= 4\\left(3 + 4\\right) \\\\= 28.\\end{array}</eq> <eq>\\begin{array}{l}\\frac{46 + 28}{= 4\\ (4 + 7)} \\\\= 44.\\end{array}</eq></td></tr><tr><td>Adding two even numbers that are not multiples of 4 will always give a multiple of 4 because their remainders of 2 add up to 4.</td><td><eq>( 4 p + 2 ) \\; \\mathrm { a n d } \\; ( 4 q + 2 ) .</eq> <eq>\\begin{array} { r l } &amp; { ( 4 p + 2 ) + ( 4 q + 2 ) } \\\\ &amp; { = 4 p + 4 q + 4 } \\\\ &amp; { = 4   ( p + q + 1 ) . } \\end{array}</eq></td><td><img src=\"images/page_5_table_image_5_3.jpg\"/></td><td><img src=\"images/page_5_table_image_5_4.jpg\"/></td><td>2, 6, 10, 18, 22,42. <eq>2 + 6 = 8.</eq> <eq>\\begin{array} { l } 6 + 1 0 = 1 6 . \\\\ 2 2 + 6 = 2 8 . \\end{array}</eq></td></tr></table>"
